{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1414842-paradox-only-around-sleepers","id":1414842,"title":"Paradox Only Around Sleepers","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-09-20T16:38:02-07:00","id":1414842,"title":"Paradox Only Around Sleepers","content":"In Awakening 2e, Paradox is no longer a thing that automatically happens except around Sleepers. A mage who's in an abandoned warehouse can toss fireballs to their heart's content. A mage can also voluntarily accept Paradox to make their spells more potent. <br /> <br /> I've long enjoyed MtAs's setting and have only played in one MtAs game. I'm toying with the idea of GMing one and adopting this setting hack. When I played my mage PC in that game, I was largely disappointed at how costly magick felt to perform. There was this disclaimer...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Beyond all of those factors, you also have several core themes of Mage: courage, imagination, hope, struggle, sacrifice, progress, consequences, and interconnectedness. None of those themes would be supported--and most, if not all, of them would be undercut--if magick were a quick and easy thing that every hero could just DO. The theme of Paradox enhances the other elements of Mage. If that doesn\u2019t work for you, then you\u2019re probably playing the wrong game. </div> </div> </div>...but in my experience, it mostly resulted in cool things I wanted my PC to do having prohibitive-feeling costs attached, so I just didn't do them. The game also didn't didn't use any chantries/sanctums/reality zones/mythic threads/excursions to the Umbra/etc., so that might have biased my thinking--there was basically no way to avoid Paradox. And when the setting's goal is to keep vulgar magick rare, Paradox succeeds, since players will usually go out of their way to avoid paying costs they don't have to pay. I'm just not sure what it adds to say no (free) vulgar magick around other mages and night-folk. Paradox around Sleepers ensures mages can't openly rule the world. <br /> <br /> So what would Ascension's setting look like if this was the case? How would games be different?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-20T19:52:04-07:00","id":1414864,"title":"","content":"Since it would require less resources to go arround casting Vulgar stuff, mages ain't going to figth for those resources. This is mostly chantries/sanctums/reality zones/mythic threads/excursions to the Umbra/etc. Sanctums in particular don't have reason to exist. The constant figth against Paradox defines much of Mage logistics. It makes mages protective of their nests, to to speak. Much of the politics of mage become less existential if you can just buy a flat and do whatever you want there.<br /> <br /> As you note, players ain't going to be thinking that much about how to cast their spells in a Coincident fashion.<br /> <br /> You will prob want to extrapolate the Revised Sanctum rules to all of reality. Those state that if you cast an obviously Vulgar spell insisde your Sanctum (like, say, transforming in a four armed godess of death), it's Coincident as long as the spell terminates the moment you go out (unless you cast it as Vulgar magick, taking the Paradox even when you're within your Sanctum).<br /> Otherwise it would be feasible to cast any and all Vulgar magick inside your cave and just let it's effects go outside afterwards to (kinda) rule the world in the open (not that the Technocracy it's going to allow this, but it would undermine the importance of first gaining the masses if u want to do this. That's an important element of the mage metaplot and setting).<br /> This is a very good rule for Sanctums as far as preserving the setting goes, and since now &quot;the world it's your Sanctum&quot; it could be reasonable to extrapolate and say free Vulgar spells terminate at the moment someone sees them<br /> <br /> One could think where this leaves Consensual reality. If one starts delving on the metaphysical implications of not having paradox as the permanent state of &quot;static reality pushing back&quot;, the setting migth unravel.<br /> For instance , the Ascencion War becomes meaningless if you can be immortal w/o Paradox as long as you cast your spells in an abandoned place because, one could think, Paradox no longer follows you wherever you go in the material plane. Thus Realms are no longer important. Thus the conflicts for Nodes become very questionable. And so on...I think this could be averted by adressing that the rules for permanent mojo do not change.<br /> <br /> One needs some way to show that even when the world migth be very lenient to temporary changes only wittnessed by Awakened creatures, it's still very much ruled by the Consensus (at least as far as magic(k) it's concerned), to justify Mage metaplot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-21T12:51:32-07:00","id":1414953,"title":"","content":"I'd regulate as follows: there's only coincidental and vulgar with witnesses, so difficulty is either +3 or +5, and paradox gain is sphere+1, double on botch.<br /> <br /> Balance wise not much changes. Mages explode less, but consensus still dictates what is easy and what is hard, and the new shenanigans aren't significantly crazier than what a careful mage can pull. It basically lowers the &quot;skill at bullshitting cap&quot; and makes the scene flashier.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-21T14:25:41-07:00","id":1414969,"title":"","content":"In <i>Mage Made Easy</i>, Phil 'Satyros' Brucato suggests subtle/uncanny/extreme as ways to see magick. Ignore witnesses when considering the effect. The difficulties are highest sphere+3, highest sphere+4 and highest sphere+5, respectively. Subtle is going to work if not used too often and if it's close to the local reality. Hence, you might get away with some stunts in an action movie if you're in an area like Hollywood. That being said, a lot of the crazy action movie stunts or magic stuff are going to be, where the locals don't believe them, uncanny. Think the crazy car stunts in the later Fast and the Furious franchise like them dropping out of aircraft compared to more subtle stunt driving early on. Extreme--well that's opening planar gates and other stuff that just isn't there. See pages 17-19 of that lighter tome by Mage's main developer for more ideas. The +3/+5 difficulty suggestions works well--if you want +4 it's for those 'it's crazy and bends the laws of physics but at least you can b.s. that'<br /> <br /> Honestly, in practice in Mage: the Ascension it is best to spend a Willpower to get a success on a roll or to avoid a botch on a magical effect. A Sanctum for crafting and for Correspondence sensing magic also stop a lot of Paradox. Vulgar magick per M20 is only 1 Paradox unless you botch, so it's something you can cast without great fear if you are careful. Keep under 5 Paradox and 1 should bleed off per week of game time. If you get more, you're getting into a lot of fights or perhaps just don't understand how to make your magic Coincidental (or subtle).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-21T16:40:42-07:00","id":1414991,"title":"","content":"As a rule, I always drop the vulgar w/o witnesses category from the game whenever I play MtA, b/c I find the result much more congenial to my need to make magic more flashy than not. It is one of my default houserules, together with rolling Arete + Sphere for any magic effect.<br /> <br /> Alternatively, a nifty little setting hack I like a lot and found in another game's incorporation of the WoD and MtA in a meta-setting is this one (there are others I fancy, but they seem less relevant to the topic):<br /> <br /> Hot War<br /> By and large, the Ascension War is a cold war. There are skirmishes here and there, but very little front line fighting. Then you selected this. The Ascension War is hot. The Traditions,Technocracy, and Nephandi are engaged in open warfare. Vulgar will working is frequently being hurled in the streets in broad daylight, chantries and constructs are under siege, and entire realms burn on the Horizon. The war pushes reality to its breaking point and consensual reality world wide becomes accepting of the sorts of feats you could find in low-level science fiction, urban fantasy, and horror genre works.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 09-22-2020, 10:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-22T10:09:59-07:00","id":1415078,"title":"","content":"This is my current set of M20 houserules. Most of them seem relevant for the topic at hand.<br /> <br /> Backgrounds can be bought and raised with experience points.<br /> Avatar Storm and Disembodiment do not exist.<br /> The maximum amount of Quintessence that may be expended to lower magickal difficulties is the highest of Avatar or Prime.<br /> The magickal pool is Arete + highest Sphere involved in the effect.<br /> Everybody important can soak lethal damage like bashing.<br /> Final modifier to magickal rolls cannot exceed +/- 3; all modifiers, including Abilities enhancing magick and Quintessence expenditure, can be combined to reach the maximum net amount.<br /> <br /> Magickal effects can be Hidden, Disguised, or Defiant. Hidden magick is any effect that is cast when not in the presence of Sleepers, or that the Sleeper population around the mage accepts as normal or fails to notice. Effects that are accepted as a believable event by the local Sleeper population are considered Hidden magick. It also includes those effects that would be surely recognized as magical if there were a Sleeper witness but there is none at the moment. For Hidden magick, the creator does no effort to cloak it, and only cares about the success of the effect, so it is the easiest to cast. Yet it is fairly safe because the effect is part of the local Consensus, there is no Sleeper around, or they are not in a position to acknowledge it. In the absence of Sleeper witnesses to reinforce it, local reality usually has to yield to the Awakened will and power of a mage (if they properly shape the effect) and accept magick as possible. Therefore Hidden magick causes no Paradox if it is successful.<br /> <br /> Disguised magick takes place when one or more Sleepers witness and notice it, but are fooled about its true nature. When casting a Disguised effect, the mage shapes it so that Sleeper observers interpret it as a normal, if unusual, event or the product of chance, or the effect itself makes them unable to notice. This kind of effect happens when Sleeper observers are present and would notice the magick but the mage makes an extra conscious effort to disguise it as a normal event. This may be done by shaping the magick to appear as something that could conceivably happen, or by cloaking it with a conjunctional illusion or distraction effect. In casting Disguised magick, mages choose to make an additional cloaking effort, hence they suffer a higher difficulty. However it does not cause Paradox if successful.<br /> Defiant magick occurs when there are Sleeper witnesses that notice it and deem it an impossible event according to their paradigm. It is not so easy as Hidden effects (Sleeper presence makes local reality much less flexible to Awakened will) nor so complex as Disguised magick (the mage does not have to care about cloaking the effect) but it carries a serious Paradox penalty that grows ever heavier the greater the number of Sleeper observers.<br /> <br /> Base Casting Roll<br /> Effect is\u2026 Difficulty<br /> Defiant 7<br /> Disguised 8<br /> Hidden 6<br /> <br /> Paradox Points Generated<br /> On a Success<br /> Defiant One point + one per factor of ten in Sleeper witnesses<br /> Disguised None<br /> Hidden None<br /> <br /> On a Botch<br /> Defiant Two + Two per dot in highest Sphere + two per factor of ten in Sleeper witnesses<br /> Disguised One per dot in highest Sphere<br /> Hidden One per dot in highest Sphere<br /> <br /> Paradox Backlash Roll<br /> Storyteller rolls one die for each point of Paradox in character\u2019s current Paradox pool, against difficulty 6.<br /> Successes Effects of Discharge<br /> Botch All Paradox points discharge harmlessly.<br /> No successes No effects, but no Paradox points discharge.<br /> 1-5 One point of Paradox discharged per success. Mage also suffers a Burn or acquires a trivial Paradox Flaw.<br /> 6-10 One point of Paradox discharged per success. Mage also suffers a Burn or acquires a minor Paradox Flaw.<br /> 11-15 Usual Paradox discharge, as well as one of the following effects: a Burn; a significant Paradox Flaw; a Paradox Spirit visitation; or a mild Quiet.<br /> 16-20 Usual Paradox discharge, as well as two of the following effects: a Burn; a severe Paradox Flaw; a Paradox Spirit visitation; a moderate Quiet; or banishment to a Paradox Realm.<br /> 21+ Usual Paradox discharge, as well as two of the following effects: a Burn; one drastic Paradox Flaw; a Paradox Spirit visitation; a severe Quiet; or banishment to a Paradox Realm.<br /> <br /> A Burn is an harmful discharge of Paradox energies that may inflict one of the following: one level of Bashing damage per Paradox point discharged; one level of Lethal damage per two Paradox points discharged (round down); or one level of Aggravated damage per three Paradox points or fraction therein. The player can usually choose which form Paradox damage takes. All Paradox damage can be soaked with Arete + Avatar. The player may be often able to substitute Avatar with an appropriate Sphere, such as Prime, Life, Forces, Entropy, or Mind, depending on the form the harmful discharge of Paradox takes.<br /> <br /> Common manifestations of Paradox typically take the form of the Burn, Paradox Flaws, Quiet, Paradox Spirit visitations, or banishment to a Paradox Realm. In almost any case, Paradox manifestation never have a permanent duration, short of a mage having lasting alterations to one\u2019s pattern that are noticeable to Sleepers and seemingly impossible according to local Consensus.<br /> <br /> A few ways do exist to shed Paradox, but they all require forswearing magick use and/or active presence in the world for significant amounts of time, or alternatively advanced use of Prime and Quintessence expenditure.<br /> <b>Bleeding</b>: given time, Paradox energies bleed off and fade away from the mage\u2019s pattern. It takes one week per Paradox point for the energy to bleed off, provided that the mage earns no more Paradox during that time.<br /> <b>Abstinence</b>: if the mage avoids doing magick for a time, the Paradox bleeds off at double speed: one point every three days. Any magick cast during that time immediately undoes the process, leaving the Paradox Pool at its previous level.<br /> <b>Purification</b>: the mage has to retire from all companionship, attune himself to the universe and the self, and perform some sort of cleansing or centering ritual (prayer, fasting, bathing, trance, meditation, athletic or mnemonic exercises, mathematical calculations, poetry composition). The character has to meditate alone for one day per point in their Paradox Pool. The mage cannot do anything else but attend to basic needs during this time. Otherwise, the character loses any benefit from the purification ritual and the Paradox Pool returns at its previous level.<br /> <b>Nullification</b>: Masters of Prime can use a Prime 5 effect to channel Quintessence and purify their pattern from Paradox with the energies of Creation. The character has to expend Quintessence to remove Paradox on a one-point-per-each-point basis.<br /> <br /> All Effects cast within a Sanctum by its owner and other mages with compatible paradigms are considered Hidden magick, regardless of the presence of Sleepers. A Sanctum reduces the difficulty of spells performed in its area by half its Background\u2019s rating (round down). The Effects of rival mages with incompatible paradigms to the area\u2019s prevailing reality have to be cast as Defiant or Disguised magick, but only as long as the owner is present and able to notice them. A Sanctum has an Arcane rating equivalent to the place\u2019s Background rating. As long as they stay within the protected area, characters enjoy the protection of this Arcane effect too. (A character who <i>already </i>has the Arcane Background gets whichever Arcane rating is highest \u2013 the rating of the Sanctum or the rating of her Arcane rating \u2013 not a combination of both.). The Gauntlet is one level lower in mystic Sanctums and one level higher in a tecnomagickal Laboratory than it would be otherwise. And a Technocracy Laboratory has a Gauntlet rating of 9 except with regards to Dimensional Science procedures, whose Effects are considered Hidden in an appropriate Technocratic Lab.<br /> <br /> For mystics, Arete \u2022\u2022 makes all instruments optional for one-dot Effects; Arete \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 makes them optional for two-dot Effects; and so on, with every two dots of Arete pushing the number of dots where instruments are optional up by one. For technomystics, you start the process at Arete \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 \u2022 and push the threshold up by one for every dot of Arete.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-22T10:26:22-07:00","id":1415080,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1414842-paradox-only-around-sleepers?p=1414864#post1414864\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One could think where this leaves Consensual reality. If one starts delving on the metaphysical implications of not having paradox as the permanent state of &quot;static reality pushing back&quot;, the setting migth unravel.</div> </div> </div> </div>I fail to see where the real thematic and metaphysical difficulty lies. You may easily assume (as I do for my houserules) that the Consensus by itself is always weaker than properly focused Awakened will unless reinforced by the active intervention of its Sleeper creators. Therefore, Paradox cannot exist unless the mage bungles the spell or Sleepers are present to notice and disbelieve the effect.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For instance , the Ascencion War becomes meaningless if you can be immortal w/o Paradox as long as you cast your spells in an abandoned place because, one could think, Paradox no longer follows you wherever you go in the material plane. Thus Realms are no longer important. Thus the conflicts for Nodes become very questionable. And so on...I think this could be averted by adressing that the rules for permanent mojo do not change. </div> </div> </div>Personally I much prefer if immortality is the default state of sufficiently powerful and successful mages, elder mages are as present and active in the setting as elder vampires, and Realms and Nodes are only relevant (but still quite useful) for mages as supply and base resources in the Ascension War. From my PoV the consequences you describe are welcome benefits, not problems.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> One needs some way to show that even when the world migth be very lenient to temporary changes only wittnessed by Awakened creatures, it's still very much ruled by the Consensus (at least as far as magic(k) it's concerned), to justify Mage metaplot. </div> </div> </div>Mages still need to win the Ascension War and shape the Consensus to their liking in order to use obvious magic safely and frequently. This IMO seems more than motivation enough to justify the conflict and the Mage metaplot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 09-22-2020, 01:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-22T11:26:09-07:00","id":1415092,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1414842-paradox-only-around-sleepers?p=1415080#post1415080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I fail to see where the real thematic and metaphysical difficulty lies. You may easily assume (as I do for my houserules) that the Consensus by itself is always weaker than properly focused Awakened will unless reinforced by the active intervention of its Sleeper creators. Therefore, Paradox cannot exist unless the mage bungles the spell or Sleepers are present to notice and disbelieve the effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>You could assume that, However, it has to be adressed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Personally I much prefer if immortality is the default state of sufficiently powerful and successful mages, elder mages are as present and active in the setting as elder vampires, and Realms and Nodes are only relevant (but still quite useful) for mages as supply and base resources in the Ascension War. From my PoV the consequences you describe are welcome benefits, not problems. </div> </div> </div>However that does change the setting a lot. That being my point when I say &quot;the setting colapses&quot; (perhaps I was too dramatic?). The OP asked how does this affect the setting. Well, it may change the setting quite a lot.<br /> <br /> That doesn't need to be a bad thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mages still need to win the Ascension War and shape the Consensus to their liking in order to use obvious magic safely and frequently. This IMO seems more than motivation enough to justify the conflict and the Mage metaplot. </div> </div> </div>If &quot;immortality is the default state of sufficiently powerful and successful mages&quot; one of the main reasons for the Ascencion War (the reason &quot;elders&quot; do it) it's lost to everyone but the most megalomaniac. And the technocracy of course, because they want the mosters gone (and, actually, the technocracy has as one of it's main founding fathers a guy that just wanted to be immortal too, soo...it's kind of a big deal).<br /> <br /> As far as safety goes: It's always going to be much safer not to figth. If you figth, you court death (this is always true, but more so if you're not a Technocrat - it's not <i>safe</i> to figth them, it's not a <i>nice </i>figth either).<br /> I fail to see why wizards would expose themselves to the Ascencion War if no amount of Consensus it's going to ruin their mojo as long as they avoid to cast spells arround normal people.<br /> <br /> Having the existential threat of Paradox hanging on your neck does a lot to explain why Mages would accept being the underdog in a fight without quarter against much more powerful foes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 09-22-2020, 02:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-22T13:38:45-07:00","id":1415112,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1414842-paradox-only-around-sleepers?p=1415092#post1415092\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If &quot;immortality is the default state of sufficiently powerful and successful mages&quot; one of the main reasons for the Ascencion War (the reason &quot;elders&quot; do it) it's lost to everyone but the most megalomaniac. And the technocracy of course, because they want the mosters gone (and, actually, the technocracy has as one of it's main founding fathers a guy that just wanted to be immortal too, soo...it's kind of a big deal).</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, allow me to express my abundant skepticism easy immortality spells alone would be sufficient to defuse or substantially tone down the Ascension War. If one looks closely to Mage history in its entirety, it is plain that fighting between rival mage factions had been widespread in a ptachwork fashion across the world (esp. in Europe, the Middle East, and South/East Asia) centuries and millennia before the Order of Reason formed and started its big purge. The advent of the Technocracy just drove the conflict to get more vicious and consolidate into a continuous four-way war. Moreover, the vampires have been waging a millennail fratricidal war of their own that is just as vicious as the Ascension War in its own way (just a bit less... direct, but that's a result of the difference between the Kindred and the Awakened power set). Yet elder vampires have immortality by default, and no apparent better justification for fighting than getting a distraction from the drawbacks of their immortality mode.<br /> <br /> Elder mages have less flawed means of immortality esp. with this hack (please take into account that canon history of the vampirization of House Tremere IMO makes no story or metaphysical sense, it is just a very flawed legacy of obsolete ties with Ars Magica, so I ignore it entirely) but they balance it with an innate drive to reshape the world in the image of their paradigm, which was blatant way before Paradox started to give trouble to immortal mages. The Technocracy is still going to retain its genocidal drive to purge rival mages and troublesome supernaturals, and the Traditions are still going to organize and fight back as a consequence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As far as safety goes: It's always going to be much safer not to figth. If you figth, you court death (this is always true, but more so if you're not a Technocrat - it's not <i>safe</i> to figth them, it's not a <i>nice </i>figth either). </div> </div> </div>The vampires and up to a point, the faeries too were immortal by default, yet their own history has no lack of violent and vicious conflicts between rival factions. Safety concerns never stopped the supernaturals from trying to wipe each other out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I fail to see why wizards would expose themselves to the Ascencion War if no amount of Consensus it's going to ruin their mojo as long as they avoid to cast spells arround normal people. </div> </div> </div>The advent of the Technocracy and the Traditions just made the recurrent fighting between mage factions much more organized and global, but it existed in a patchwork fashion at least since the European/Middle Eastern progenitors of the Order of Reason and the Traditions first took shape in Ancient Egypt.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Having the existential threat of Paradox hanging on your neck does a lot to explain why Mages would accept being the underdog in a fight without quarter against much more powerful foes. </div> </div> </div>Those foes having an overwhelming ideological drive and organization effort to exterminate all rival factions is abundant motivation to fight a war of survival. The Order of Reason formed and started its big purge, forcing the Traditions to band together and fight back, at least three centuries before the Consensus started shifting to give serious Paradox trouble to Tradition magic.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 09-22-2020, 04:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-22T13:57:53-07:00","id":1415116,"title":"","content":"I think in a setting where immortality was commonplace for mages you would see a sociopolitical setup more like Vampire: The Masquerade's. Vampires risk their immortal existences on the front lines of Camarilla vs. Sabbat and Anarchs vs. elders wars, but it's usually younger vampires who are doing so at the behest of their elders. Elder mages would have incentive to throw younger mages into the Ascension War as cannon fodder, because the elders aren't ever going to get old and retire from their positions, and the younger mages will try to seize power unless they're distracted by an external enemy and the promise of social advancement contingent on success in the war effort. You would also likely see a lot more masters and archmasters hanging around when old age doesn't kill them off.<br /> <br /> The Ascension War still makes a lot of sense to me in a Paradox-lite setting where more mages are immortal, but that also makes the setting feel too similar to Vampire's for my tastes. Mage should be its own thing. <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> makes some good points on the setting impacts that reduced Paradox would have. I think I will be inclined to run Mage out of the book, and to simply make chantries/sanctums/reality zones/mythic threads/excursions to the Umbra/etc. more commonplace than they were during my own play experience. If that still makes magick feel too restrictive, I can always remove &quot;vulgar without witnesses&quot; Paradox later. Players usually feel happier about accepting a power-up than a power-down too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-22T14:27:11-07:00","id":1415118,"title":"","content":"Well, I think that depends on how do you do it. You could drop &quot; vulgar w/o witnesses&quot; w/o affecting rules about immortality, or any other rules. These are different kinds of Paradox<br /> <br /> There are more than one way to spin this, Paradox being less explosive w/o sleepers doesn't need to mean Paradox doesn't exist under that circumstance (albeit it could).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 09-22-2020, 05:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-22T15:17:59-07:00","id":1415123,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>False Epiphany</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1414842-paradox-only-around-sleepers?p=1415116#post1415116\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think in a setting where immortality was commonplace for mages you would see a sociopolitical setup more like Vampire: The Masquerade's. Vampires risk their immortal existences on the front lines of Camarilla vs. Sabbat and Anarchs vs. elders wars, but it's usually younger vampires who are doing so at the behest of their elders. Elder mages would have incentive to throw younger mages into the Ascension War as cannon fodder, because the elders aren't ever going to get old and retire from their positions, and the younger mages will try to seize power unless they're distracted by an external enemy and the promise of social advancement contingent on success in the war effort. You would also likely see a lot more masters and archmasters hanging around when old age doesn't kill them off.<br /> <br /> The Ascension War still makes a lot of sense to me in a Paradox-lite setting where more mages are immortal, but that also makes the setting feel too similar to Vampire's for my tastes. Mage should be its own thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>YMMV, but IMO the main thematic differences between Vampire and Mage have little to do and would still stand even with the changes you describe in the Ascension War. They are mostly related to the different metaphysical natures of vampires and mages, not with immortality being more or less easy for the Awakened. Vampires are trapped into ennui and stasis, mages are agents of change and evolution. Vampires tend to fight their wars in a less direct and more mundane, if just as vicious, way than mages due to their different power set. Moreover, the split you describe largely characterizes the canon Ascension War to a good degree anyway. Elder mages tend to stay sheltered away from the frontlines. It is irrelevant whether it happens to make their immortality spells more reliable or to keep them safe from fighting. I find the whole Avatar Storm and Disembodiment d\u00e9b\u00e2cle obnoxious nonsense since the onset of the edition wars and go out of my way to ignore it, as M20 allows to do. Moreover, the leadership of the elder vampires in the Jyhad tends to be erratic due to the torpor issue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 09-23-2020, 03:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-22T15:24:27-07:00","id":1415125,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1414842-paradox-only-around-sleepers?p=1415118#post1415118\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, I think that depends on how do you do it. You could drop &quot; vulgar w/o witnesses&quot; w/o affecting rules about immortality, or any other rules. These are different kinds of Paradox<br /> <br /> There are more than one way to spin this, Paradox being less explosive w/o sleepers doesn't need to mean Paradox doesn't exist under that circumstance (albeit it could).</div> </div> </div> </div>I concede my viewpoint is strongly biased in favor of mage immortality just like flashy magic, no Paradox for it is one of the rule and setting hacks I always go out of my way to enforce like removal of vulgar w/o witnesses, but this seems metaphysical grasping straws to me. Barring rare circumstances, Paradox needing present and explicit Sleeper disbelief to work naturally leads to its leaving immortality spells alone, b/c in normal circumstances being immortal is not something a casual observer can notice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 09-22-2020, 06:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-24T12:23:52-07:00","id":1415308,"title":"","content":"Wait so Vulgar only in front of witness is not the default? We started in M20 and understood it always that vulgar means you are doing some visitable realityhacking in front of witnesses with the exception of time travel, that is always problematic because it is some deep changing shit like trying to turn the earth flat or geocentric..","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>As I am from Austria I need to clarify two things.<br /> First my native language is german and so please point out if the english I write is broken so I can improve.<br /> Second I do not own VTMV beyond first three books nor any line after M20 Corebook because it is not out there and I wait for the translation.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26160-koronus","name":"Koronus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}